<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tiananmen Square massacre, 1989 Tiananmen Square protests, Tiananmen Square protests</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square protests were student-led demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and other Chinese cities from April to June 1989, calling for political reform, freedom of the press and an end to corruption. The government declared martial law, and on the night of 3–4 June the army cleared Tiananmen Square and the surrounding streets, killing hundreds and possibly thousands of people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1989-tiananmen-square-protests-and-massacre.jpg" alt="1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre" width="299" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jiří Tondl (Blow up) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ud%C3%A1losti_na_n%C3%A1m%C4%9Bst%C3%AD_Tian_an_men,_%C4%8C%C3%ADna_1989,_foto_Ji%C5%99%C3%AD_Tondl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s death in 1976 and the arrest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> launched a program of economic reform called reform and opening up. He promoted allies to top posts: Zhao Ziyang became Premier in 1980 and Hu Yaobang became General Secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in 1982. The reforms dismantled Mao-era guarantees such as lifetime employment in state-owned enterprises, the so-called iron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> bowl, which contributed to worker unrest over layoffs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>. Common grievances by 1989 included inflation, corruption, the limited preparedness of graduates for the new economy and restrictions on political participation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The protests began after the sudden death of Hu Yaobang from a heart attack on 15 April 1989, which many students linked to his forced resignation. Mourners gathered around the Monument to the People's Heroes in Tiananmen Square, and within days the posters on campuses spoke of corruption, democracy and freedom of the press. Student protesters called for greater political accountability, constitutional due process and freedom of speech, while workers' protests centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and the economic reforms. At the height, on 17 May, about one million people were assembled in the square. A student-led hunger strike in May spread the protests to some 400 cities.</p><p>The leadership was divided. Zhao Ziyang favoured dialogue and a soft approach, while Premier Li Peng argued that stability overrode all else; they clashed at a meeting on 1 May. The State Council declared martial law on 20 May and mobilised at least 30 divisions, but crowds in the suburbs blocked the army, and it withdrew on 24 May. A meeting of the party leadership on 1 June decided to clear the square. Troops advanced into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on the early morning of 4 June and clashed with demonstrators trying to block them, and most of the killings took place along Chang'an Avenue in the western suburbs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The death toll is disputed. An official announcement soon afterwards put it at about 300, and the Beijing Party Secretary Li Ximing reported a confirmed toll of 241 on 19 June. Other estimates range from several hundred to the low thousands, with thousands more wounded. On 13 June the Beijing Public Security Bureau ordered the arrest of 21 student leaders.</p><p>Western governments imposed arms embargoes, and the government purged officials thought sympathetic to the protests and built up its riot-control forces. Political reforms begun in 1986 halted, and market-oriented economic reforms were later accelerated after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s southern tour in 1992. The Chinese government calls the events the political turmoil between the spring and summer of 1989, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> they are known as the June Fourth Incident.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1982, collective leadership and retirement norms brought more predictable government under Jiang Zemin and Hu Jintao, but the party kept its monopoly of power, crushing the <b>Tiananmen Square protests</b> on 4 June 1989.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Goddess of Democracy raised during the <b>Tiananmen Square protests</b> of 1989 was inspired by French democratic traditions, though its sculptors took care to avoid imitating the statue.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese students broadcast it during the <b>Tiananmen Square protests</b> of 1989, and on Christmas Day 1989, after the fall of the Berlin Wall, Leonard Bernstein conducted it in Berlin with choir and orchestra members from East and West Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a></li></ul></section>
